<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Tool-integrated reasoning (TIR) is difficult to scale to small language models due to instability in long-horizon tool interactions and limited model capacity. While reinforcement learning methods like group relative policy optimization provide only sparse outcome-level rewards. Recently, on-policy distillation (OPD) has gained popularity by supplying dense token-level supervision from a teacher on student-generated trajectories. However, our experiments indicate that applying OPD to TIR leads to a critical failure mode: erroneous tool calls tend to cascade across subsequent reasoning steps, progressively amplifying student-teacher divergence and rendering the teacher’s token-level supervision increasingly unreliable.
To address this, we propose SOD, a step-wise on-policy distillation framework for small language model agents, which adaptively reweights distillation strength at each step based on step-level divergence. Therefore, SOD can attenuate potentially misleading teacher signals in high-divergence regions while preserving dense guidance in well-aligned states. Experiments on challenging math, science, and code benchmarks show that SOD achieves up to <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">20.86%</span> improvement over the second-best baseline. <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notably, our 0.6B student achieves 26.13% on average@32 at AIME 2025</span>, demonstrating effective transfer of agentic reasoning to lightweight models.
Our code is available at <a href="https://github.com/YoungZ365/SOD" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/YoungZ365/SOD</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic capabilities have substantially expanded the applicability of large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, enabling them to solve complex real-world tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
However, such capabilities are typically realized by large-scale models, which incurs substantial inference cost, deployment overhead, and system complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. In latency-, resource-, and privacy-sensitive scenarios, transferring agentic capabilities to small language models (SLMs) that can be deployed on-device is therefore of significant practical importance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Despite this motivation, enabling SLMs to acquire stable and effective tool-integrated reasoning (TIR) abilities remains a major challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing post-training methods for enhancing TIR abilities are largely based on reinforcement learning (RL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, particularly policy optimization algorithms such as group relative policy optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. However, in SLM-based TIR settings, RL often suffers from unstable optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. TIR tasks typically involve long-horizon trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, multi-step decision making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and interactions with external tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, whereas RL commonly provides only sparse outcome-level rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. For small models with limited capacity and weaker exploration ability, such sparse supervision can further exacerbate exploration failure, leaving the policy in a cold-start regime with few informative reasoning signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, on-policy distillation (OPD) has emerged as a promising paradigm for post-training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Unlike RL methods that rely on sparse, trajectory-level rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, OPD provides dense token-level supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> on trajectories sampled from the student’s own policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, thereby alleviating the credit assignment difficulty inherent in sparse reward signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> while substantially improving sample efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> and training stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07725v1/pic/intro_v13.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/169;" width="476" height="169" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The motivation of SOD</span>.
(a) Student-teacher divergence <math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> across reasoning steps, sampled from 800 trajectories: in TIR, erroneous tool calls cause divergence to accelerate sharply, unlike the gradual drift in text-only reasoning.
(b) Teacher entropy statistics over 800 sampled trajectories: on erroneous trajectories, both the mean entropy change (bars) and the standard deviation (dashed lines) grow rapidly at later steps, indicating increasingly unreliable teacher supervision. Please refer to <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Visualization and Analysis of Token Entropy from The Teacher ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> for detailed cases.
(c) Radar chart comparing methods on four benchmarks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, our experiments show that directly transferring OPD to SLM-based TIR can lead to severe training instability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. We attribute this failure to a fundamental difference between how student trajectories deviate from the teacher distribution in TIR and in standard text-based reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. As illustrated in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), in text-only reasoning, even when the student gradually departs from the teacher, subsequent states still evolve continuously along the generated context, and the resulting distribution shift is typically progressive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. In contrast, TIR introduces discontinuous state transitions through tool interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. A single erroneous tool call can inject an incorrect observation into the context, causing subsequent reasoning steps to unfold from a corrupted state <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. This issue is further amplified because teacher models are often highly accurate in tool use, whereas small models have limited capacity and weaker exploration ability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. During early training, small models may accumulate multiple erroneous tool calls, causing their state distribution to rapidly drift away from the teacher distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. In such out-of-distribution states, teacher-provided token-level supervision may become unreliable or even misleading <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> as illustrated in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b), and the enlarged student-teacher discrepancy can induce unstable gradients and even training collapse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this observation, we propose <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SOD</span>, a
<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>tep-wise <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>n-policy <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>istillation framework for small language model agents. The core idea is to adaptively adjust the distillation strength at each reasoning step according to the divergence between the student and teacher. Specifically, when the student remains well aligned with the teacher, SOD preserves dense supervision to fully exploit teacher guidance; when the deviation becomes large, it progressively attenuates the distillation signal for the corresponding step, thereby reducing the influence of potentially misleading supervision under out-of-distribution states.
Experimental results (as displayed in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c)) demonstrate that this simple yet effective step-wise reweighting mechanism enables lightweight agents to acquire agentic TIR capabilities more efficiently.
Extensive experiments on challenging math, science, and code benchmarks show that SOD outperforms the second-best baseline by up to <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">20.86%</span>.
Notably, our 0.6B-scale student achieves <span id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">26.13%</span> accuracy on AIME 2025. To our best knowledge, this is the first sub-billion-parameter model to reach this level on such a challenging reasoning benchmark.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reinforcement Learning for Agents.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RL-based post-training has evolved from reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> with PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> to more scalable methods like GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. For language agents, structured reasoning paradigms such as ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, Toolformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and FireAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> enable tool use but rely on demonstrations rather than online optimization. Recent work extends RL to agent interaction trajectories across code generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, tool use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, GUI interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, and web navigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. A central challenge is credit assignment under sparse, delayed feedback, addressed via trajectory-level updates and value-free formulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>. KL-regularized policy optimization further introduces bias and instability concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, amplified in agentic settings by distribution shift and compounding errors. Broader frameworks scaling agentic RL across environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> still rely on trajectory-level rewards without dense supervision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">On-policy Distillation.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On-Policy Distillation (OPD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> introduces token-level supervision on student-generated trajectories to mitigate the distribution mismatch of offline distillation. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. [24]</a></cite> formulates OPD as reverse KL minimization under the student distribution, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al. [23]</a></cite> unifies on-policy and off-policy distillation across divergence objectives. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. [60]</a></cite> further interprets OPD as KL-regularized reinforcement learning with implicit per-token rewards, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [29]</a></cite> shows that OPD primarily aligns local support on student-visited states, depending on teacher–student compatibility in reasoning patterns. OPD has also been extended to self-distillation settings without external teachers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>.
Recently, more works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite> have explored introducing OPD to mitigate the limitations of RL.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Multi-turn Tool-Integrated Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the post-training of a small language model (SLM) for multi-turn tool-integrated reasoning (TIR). Given an input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the model interacts with an external environment over multiple reasoning steps. At each step <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the model generates a response <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may include natural-language reasoning, a tool invocation, or a final answer. If a tool is invoked, the environment returns an observation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is appended to the context and conditions subsequent generations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A trajectory is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(x,y_{1},o_{1},\ldots,y_{K},o_{K},y_{K+1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(x,y_{1},o_{1},\ldots,y_{K},o_{K},y_{K+1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{K+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{K+1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the final response. The policy <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> generates only model tokens, while observations <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are provided by the environment. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a generated token and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> its prefix, which may include both model outputs and tool observations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Group Relative Policy Optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> is a reinforcement learning algorithm that updates the policy using relative rewards within a group of sampled trajectories. We assume access to an outcome-level reward function <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> defined on complete trajectories. For each input <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, a group of trajectories <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from the old policy <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, each receiving reward <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=r(\tau_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=r(\tau_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The group-relative advantage is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-\mathrm{mean}(\{r_{j}\})}{\mathrm{std}(\{r_{j}\})+\epsilon_{A}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i}=\frac{r_{i}-\mathrm{mean}(\{r_{j}\})}{\mathrm{std}(\{r_{j}\})+\epsilon_{A}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i,t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{i,t}\mid y_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(y_{i,t}\mid y_{i,&lt;t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i,t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{i,t}\mid y_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(y_{i,t}\mid y_{i,&lt;t})}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the token-level importance ratio. The objective is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}=-\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}_{i}|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}_{i}}\min\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i},\mathrm{clip}(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)\hat{A}_{i}\right)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>GRPO</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}=-\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}_{i}|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}_{i}}\min\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i},\mathrm{clip}(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)\hat{A}_{i}\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes model-generated token positions.
This objective provides an on-policy learning signal based on relative trajectory performance, but supplies only sparse outcome-level rewards.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>On-policy Distillation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On-policy distillation (OPD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> is a post-training paradigm that provides dense token-level supervision on student-generated trajectories by aligning the student policy with a teacher distribution. Given a trajectory <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\sim\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\sim\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the OPD objective is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{}}=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}_{i}}\left(\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})\right)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>OPD</mi><mphantom></mphantom></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>teacher</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{}}=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}_{i}}\left(\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the student policy, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>teacher</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the teacher model, and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of model-generated token positions in the sampled trajectory. This objective corresponds to a sampled estimator of the reverse KL divergence between the student and teacher policies on student-visited states.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce SOD.
The framework of SOD is displayed in <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4.1 Failure of On-policy Distillation under Tool-Induced State Drift ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
We first analyze why on-policy distillation (OPD) can become unreliable under tool-induced state drift. We then introduce a step-level divergence score as an observable proxy for the reliability of teacher supervision, and finally formulate a step-wise reweighted OPD objective.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Failure of On-policy Distillation under Tool-Induced State Drift</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The OPD objective assumes that teacher supervision remains reliable across all student-visited states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. This assumption holds when distribution drift is gradual, but is severely violated in TIR due to discontinuous state transitions introduced by tool observations. We formalize this failure through two propositions (proofs in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Proofs for ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define the step-level mismatch as:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta_{k}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{I}_{k}|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{I}_{k}}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid y_{&lt;t})\,\big\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(\cdot\mid y_{&lt;t})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>teacher</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{I}_{k}|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{I}_{k}}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid y_{&lt;t})\,\big\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(\cdot\mid y_{&lt;t})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Discontinuous divergence amplification)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In text-only reasoning, <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{k+1}-\Delta_{k}=O(\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k+1}-\Delta_{k}=O(\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is the per-token distributional shift. In TIR, a single erroneous tool observation of length <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> causes <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{k+1}-\Delta_{k}=\Omega(m\cdot\eta_{\mathrm{tool}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>tool</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k+1}-\Delta_{k}=\Omega(m\cdot\eta_{\mathrm{tool}})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{tool}}\gg\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>tool</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{tool}}\gg\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. Under <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> consecutive tool errors, the divergence compounds super-linearly: <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{k+j}-\Delta_{k}=\Omega\!\left(\sum_{i=0}^{j-1}m_{i}\cdot\eta_{\mathrm{tool}}^{(i)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.653em;" width="0.653em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>tool</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k+j}-\Delta_{k}=\Omega\!\left(\sum_{i=0}^{j-1}m_{i}\cdot\eta_{\mathrm{tool}}^{(i)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{tool}}^{(i+1)}\geq\eta_{\mathrm{tool}}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>tool</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>tool</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{tool}}^{(i+1)}\geq\eta_{\mathrm{tool}}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Gradient SNR degradation)<span id="Thmproposition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the teacher-supported region <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}^{\epsilon}=\{v\in\mathcal{V}:\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(v\mid y_{&lt;t})\geq\epsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>teacher</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}^{\epsilon}=\{v\in\mathcal{V}:\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(v\mid y_{&lt;t})\geq\epsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the overlap <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}=\sum_{v\in S_{t}^{\epsilon}}\pi_{\theta}(v\mid y_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}=\sum_{v\in S_{t}^{\epsilon}}\pi_{\theta}(v\mid y_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, the second moment of the OPD loss satisfies <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\ell_{t}^{2}]\geq(1-\rho)\,\log^{2}(1/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\ell_{t}^{2}]\geq(1-\rho)\,\log^{2}(1/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the signal-to-noise ratio of the gradient estimator <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}(g_{t})\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>SNR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}(g_{t})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these results characterize a failure cascade specific to TIR: consecutive erroneous tool calls trigger compounding divergence amplification (Prop. <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (Discontinuous divergence amplification). ‣ 4.1 Failure of On-policy Distillation under Tool-Induced State Drift ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), progressively pushing the student away from the teacher distribution into low-overlap states where the OPD gradient becomes dominated by high-variance, uninformative contributions (Prop. <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2 (Gradient SNR degradation). ‣ 4.1 Failure of On-policy Distillation under Tool-Induced State Drift ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). This is empirically confirmed in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: divergence accelerates sharply as tool errors accumulate (a), and teacher entropy becomes elevated and unstable in subsequent steps (b). Since OPD aggregates losses uniformly across steps, it systematically overweights these corrupted signals, motivating our step-wise reweighting mechanism.
We formally show in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4 Variance Reduction under Step-wise Reweighting ‣ Appendix D Proofs for ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a> that under monotonically increasing divergence, our reweighting suppresses the weighted second moment by a factor of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O((d_{1}/d_{k})^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O((d_{1}/d_{k})^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, restoring bounded gradient SNR even in low-overlap states where OPD suffers SNR collapse.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07725v1/pic/framework7.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/209;" width="476" height="209" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The overview of SOD</span>. (a) The student generates multi-step trajectories where erroneous tool calls propagate across steps, degrading teacher supervision reliability. (b) Student-teacher distributions drift apart as errors accumulate. (c) Step-level divergence <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies this drift. (d) SOD adaptively attenuates distillation weights in high-divergence steps, unlike vanilla OPD which applies uniform weights. Please refer to <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Three Distillation Patterns of SOD ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a> for more characteristics of SOD.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Student-teacher Divergence and Step-wise Reweighting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We partition a generated trajectory into <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1</annotation></semantics></math> reasoning steps, where each step corresponds to a model response between two tool observations, or the final answer step. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of token positions belonging to the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th model-generated step. Tool observation tokens are excluded because they are produced by the external environment rather than by the policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define a step-level divergence score to quantify student–teacher divergence at step <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{I}_{k}|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{I}_{k}}\left|\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})\right|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>teacher</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{I}_{k}|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{I}_{k}}\left|\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The score <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a lightweight indicator of the local reliability of teacher supervision. A small <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> suggests that the student remains well aligned with the teacher distribution, while a large <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates substantial mismatch, often caused by tool-induced state drift or corrupted observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on these divergence scores, we adaptively reweight the strength of distillation across steps.
We initialize the first step with full distillation strength, <em id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. For subsequent steps, the weight is:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}=\min\!\left(\prod_{u=1}^{k-1}\frac{d_{u}+\epsilon}{d_{u+1}+\epsilon},1+\delta\right),\quad k\geq 2," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}=\min\!\left(\prod_{u=1}^{k-1}\frac{d_{u}+\epsilon}{d_{u+1}+\epsilon},1+\delta\right),\quad k\geq 2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant for stability and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> controls the maximum amplification of the distillation.
Since the reweighting in <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.2 Student-teacher Divergence and Step-wise Reweighting ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> depends only on the <em id="S4.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ratios</em> between consecutive divergence scores rather than their absolute values, any monotone proxy of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> suffices. We show in <a href="#A4.SS5" title="D.5 Justification of 𝑑_𝑘 as a Proxy for Δ_𝑘 ‣ Appendix D Proofs for ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.5</span></a> that <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is monotonically consistent with <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and computable at zero marginal cost from the OPD forward pass. We therefore adopt <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in place of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in our implementation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This weighting rule captures the evolution of student-teacher divergence along the trajectory. When the divergence increases, <em id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{u+1}&gt;d_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{u+1}&gt;d_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that the student is drifting away from the teacher distribution (often due to tool-induced state shift), the corresponding ratio becomes smaller than one, leading to attenuation of the distillation signal in high-mismatch regions.
Conversely, the student may partially move back toward the teacher-supported region in later steps, effectively correcting earlier deviations. We refer to this phenomenon as <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">recovery from earlier errors</span>. In such cases, when <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{u+1}&lt;d_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{u+1}&lt;d_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, the reweighting mechanism allows the distillation strength to increase accordingly, restoring informative guidance. To ensure stable optimization, the weight is upper-bounded by <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Training Objective</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a student-generated trajectory, the sampled-token OPD term at token <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\mathrm{OPD}}(y_{t})=\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>OPD</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>teacher</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\mathrm{OPD}}(y_{t})=\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of applying this term uniformly at step level, we reweight all tokens in step <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by the corresponding reliability weight <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to the step-wise OPD objective:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{step}}=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\left[\sum_{k=1}^{K+1}w_{k}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{I}_{k}}\left(\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})\right)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>OPD</mi><mi>step</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>teacher</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{step}}=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\left[\sum_{k=1}^{K+1}w_{k}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{I}_{k}}\left(\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{\mathrm{teacher}}(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t})\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The final training objective is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{step}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>GRPO</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>OPD</mi><mi>step</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{step}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>GRPO</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides sparse outcome-level rewards to drive trajectory exploration, while <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{step}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>OPD</mi><mi>step</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{step}}</annotation></semantics></math> supplies dense token-level guidance whose strength is reweighted by the student-teacher divergence, jointly enabling stable learning under tool-induced state drift.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets &amp; Benchmarks.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. [52]</a></cite>, our training data comprises a 3k high-quality SFT corpus of multi-turn reasoning trajectories curated from s1-1k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>, LeetCode, and ReTool <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, along with a 30k diverse RL dataset covering mathematical reasoning from DAPO-Math <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>, math and coding tasks from Skywork-or1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, and scientific problem solving from MegaScience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>. Please refer to <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Training Datasets. ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> for more details. We evaluate on 4 challenging benchmarks: AIME 2024/2025, GPQA-Diamond <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>, and LiveCodeBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> (See <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Benchmarks ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> for details).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Setups.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The temperature is fixed at 1.0 with nucleus sampling parameter top_p=0.6. For each problem, 32 independent samples are generated to enable a more thorough evaluation, from which average@32 are computed and finally reported (percentage). The teacher model is a Qwen3-4B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> further optimized with GRPO on the RL datasets.
If not otherwise specified, the teacher of subsequent experiments is the 4B model. We use Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen3-1.7B as student models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines &amp; Implementation Details.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the baselines,
we evaluate SOD against a set of supervised, RL, and distillation baselines.
For each student, we compare SOD with the following baselines:
(1) Vanilla.
(2) SFT.
(3) GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
(4) OPD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
(5) OPSD<sub id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gt</span></sub> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> (On-policy self-distillation with groundtruth).
(6) OPSD<sub id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hint</span></sub> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> (On-policy self-distillation with hints).
For more details about the baselines, please refer to <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Baselines ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.
For implementation details, please refer to <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Implementation Details. ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ SOD: Step-wise On-policy Distillation for Small Language Model Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>
Performance comparison of Qwen3 teacher and student across 4 benchmarks. The best results are in <span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>, and the second-best results are <span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>. We report average@32 over 5 runs.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:282.9pt;height:130.3pt;vertical-align:-63.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-109.1pt,50.3pt) scale(0.6,0.6) ;">
<p id="S5.T1.6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:501.2pt;height:230.8pt;vertical-align:-112.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2" style="padding:0.4pt 6.5pt;"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Params</span></span>
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2" style="padding:0.4pt 6.5pt;"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></span>
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt ltx_colspan ltx_colspan_2" style="padding:0.4pt 6.5pt;"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Math</span></span>
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.4pt 6.5pt;"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Science</span></span>
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.4pt 6.5pt;"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code</span></span>
<span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2" style="padding:0.4pt 6.5pt;"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average</span></span></span>
